        "presentation": {
            "explanation": "Extended family vocabulary in Russian is deeply connected to Russia's unique **Patronymic System (Отчество)**.\n\n1. **Grandparents & Grandchildren:**\n- **дедушка** [DYE-doosh-kah] (masculine, despite -a!) / informal: *деда* — Grandfather\n- **бабушка** [BAH-boosh-kah] / informal: *баба* — Grandmother\n- **дедушка и бабушка** — Grandparents\n- **внук** [VNOOK] — Grandson\n- **внучка** [VNOOCH-kah] — Granddaughter\n- **внуки** [VNOO-kee] — Grandchildren\n\n2. **Aunts, Uncles & Cousins:**\n- **дядя** [DYAH-dyah] (masculine!) — Uncle\n- **тётя** [TYOH-tyah] — Aunt\n- **двоюродный брат** [dvah-YOO-rahd-nyy BRAHT] — Male cousin (literally: 'two-household brother')\n- **двоюродная сестра** [dvah-YOO-rahd-nah-yah see-STRAH] — Female cousin\n- **племянник** [plee-MYAHN-neek] — Nephew / **племянница** [plee-MYAHN-nee-tsah] — Niece\n\n3. **The Russian 3-Part Name & Patronymic (Отчество):**\nEvery Russian full name consists of 3 parts: **First Name + Patronymic (Father's Name) + Surname**:\n- If the father is **Иван** (Ivan):\n  * Son's patronymic: **Иванович** (Ivanovich [ee-VAH-nah-veech] = son of Ivan)\n  * Daughter's patronymic: **Ивановна** (Ivanovna [ee-VAH-nahv-nah] = daughter of Ivan)\n- If the father is **Александр** (Aleksandr):\n  * Aleksandrovich / Aleksandrovna.\n- **Polite Formal Address:** In professional, academic, and polite social life, adults are addressed by **First Name + Patronymic** (e.g. *Здравствуйте, Анна Ивановна! / Добрый день, Пётр Сергеевич!*).",
            "examples": [
                "Мой дедушка и моя бабушка живут в деревне под Новгородом.",
                "Мой дядя Сергей — брат моего отца.",
                "Это моя двоюродная сестра Ольга. Она учится в университете.",
                "Познакомьтесь, это наш профессор, Михаил Борисович."
            ],
            "mnemonics": [
                "Dedushka (Grandpa) & Dyadya (Uncle) are MASCULINE (Moy dedushka, Moy dyadya!).",
                "Patronymic endings: -ovich / -evich for men; -ovna / -evna for women.",
                "Dvoyurodnyy brat = Cousin (literally '2nd-degree brother')."
            ],
            "culturalNotes": [
                "Addressing a teacher, boss, or doctor by 'First Name + Patronymic' (Имя-Отчество) is the highest standard of Russian polite etiquette."
            ]
        },
