            "explanation": "Aleksandr Nikolayevich Sokurov (1951–present - Podorvikha, Irkutsk & St. Petersburg) and the absolute peak of historical and philosophical long-take cinema:\n- **Александр Сокуров**:\n  - Protege of Andrei Tarkovsky; winner of the **Золотой лев (Golden Lion) at the Venice Film Festival (2011)** for *Faust*; established the legendary Kabardino-Balkarian film workshop (mentoring Kantemir Balagov and Kira Kovalenko).\n- The Monumental Technical & Artistic Miracle: **«Русский ковчег» (Russian Ark - 2002)**:\n  - **The First Single-Take Feature in Film History**:\n    - A continuous **96-minute unbroken single Steadicam shot** filmed through 33 staterooms of the **Winter Palace / State Hermitage Museum** in St. Petersburg on December 23, 2001;\n    - Operated by Steadicam master Tilman Büttner, capturing 2,000 costumed actors and three live symphony orchestras without a single hidden cut or digital splice;\n    - **The Philosophical Journey through 300 Years of History**:\n      - An invisible contemporary narrator (voiced by Sokurov himself) wanders alongside the 19th-century French aristocrat **Marquis de Custine** (*The European*);\n      - They walk through time: encountering Peter the Great beating an officer, Catherine the Great rushing to her theater, Nicholas I receiving the Persian imperial apology, the 900-day Siege of Leningrad, culminating in the dazzling Imperial Grand Ball of 1913 playing Glinka's mazurka, before the crowd dissolves into mist overlooking the gray Neva River;\n      - Concludes with the immortal metaphysical line: **«И плыть нам вечно, и жить нам вечно...»** (*\"And we are destined to drift forever, and to live forever...\"*);\n  - **The Tetralogy of Power (Тетралогия власти)**: *Moloch* (Hitler - 1999), *Taurus* (Lenin - 2001), *The Sun* (Hirohito - 2005), and *Faust* (2011 - Venice Golden Lion).",
